ABSTRACT = (
    "No instrument describes the vulnerable brain of a patient with a brain tumor. This scoping review mapped the literature to propose a working concept of tumor-associated brain frailty, "
    "chart its candidate domains, and ask whether neuroinflammation and perioperative brain injury are shared mechanisms in malignant and benign tumors. We followed Joanna Briggs Institute guidance and the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses extension for scoping reviews "
    "and searched six sources to March 2026. Evidence was mapped and tallied by direction of effect without pooling. From 2,476 records, 76 studies were included, "
    "70 in a core evidence base and 6 in an instrument-focused stratum on the Indonesian Montreal Cognitive Assessment and Neurologic Assessment in Neuro-Oncology scale. "
    "No core study defined brain frailty. Frailty indices appeared in 7 core studies, cognitive testing in 30, neurological outcomes in 16, and imaging in 32. Cognition was impaired before surgery in all 8 studies that tested it, "
    "and new diffusion-weighted lesions followed 19% to 46% of resections. S100B, glial fibrillary acidic protein, and neurofilament light chain rose after surgery, "
    "but no core study reported meningioma-specific cytokine data. None used the Indonesian instruments. Tumor-associated brain frailty is best framed as a four-domain construct "
    "(systemic, cognitive, neurological, and imaging) with a two-hit course, in which the tumor is the first hit and perioperative brain injury the second. The evidence is observational, and the interaction between the hits is untested. "
    "Prospective cohorts that pair preoperative profiling with postoperative injury markers are the next step."
)
KEYWORDS = "Biomarkers, Brain Neoplasms, Frailty, Neuroinflammatory Diseases, Postoperative Cognitive Complications"

